// New review/book questions, never past-paper provenance.
// Each row: stable slug, section, difficulty, prompt, correct answer, three distractors,
// teaching explanation, and one specific explanation per distractor. Build rotates options.
export const nutritionPractice = [
  ["dri-population","foundations",3,"A survey estimates the prevalence of inadequate usual nutrient intake in a healthy population. Which reference is generally more appropriate than counting everyone below the RDA as deficient?","EAR",["UL","RDA as a diagnostic cutoff","EER for every micronutrient"],"The EAR represents the requirement of half the defined group and supports population assessment under the method's assumptions. An intake below RDA alone does not diagnose deficiency.",["UL assesses potential excess, not the prevalence of inadequacy.","The RDA deliberately exceeds most individuals' requirements; using it as a deficiency cutoff overestimates inadequacy.","EER concerns energy balance, not a requirement reference for every micronutrient."]],
  ["ai-interpretation","foundations",3,"A nutrient has an AI because the evidence cannot establish an EAR. A person's recorded intake is below that AI. What conclusion is justified?","The intake alone cannot establish inadequacy or deficiency",["The person is definitely deficient","The AI is exactly half the RDA","The AI and EAR must be added"],"An AI is a planning reference under uncertainty. Intake below it is not a validated individual deficiency diagnosis; assess usual intake and clinical context.",["This overinterprets an intake threshold without an established requirement distribution.","An AI is not a fixed fraction of an RDA.","AI and EAR are not additive targets."]],
  ["ul-target","foundations",3,"A healthy student chooses a daily supplement providing the UL, reasoning that it is the optimal intake. What is the central error?","The UL is a safety ceiling, not a nutritional goal",["The UL is the minimum that prevents deficiency","The UL is always a treatment dose","All nutrients lack toxicity above their RDA"],"The UL is the highest usual intake unlikely to pose adverse-effect risk for almost all people in the defined group. It is not a target or proof of benefit.",["The UL addresses excess, not the minimum requirement.","Clinical treatment may use different supervised doses; UL is not a prescription.","Nutrients can be harmful in excess; the RDA and UL answer different questions."]],
  ["portion-label","foundations",3,"A label gives nutrients per 30-g serving. A student eats 75 g. How should the label values be used?","Multiply each per-serving value by 2.5",["Use the listed value because the portion counts as one meal","Multiply by 0.4","Assume every portion is standardized to 30 g"],"A portion is the amount eaten. 75/30 = 2.5 labeled servings; scale energy and nutrients accordingly.",["One eating occasion can contain several standardized servings.","0.4 is the inverse ratio and would undercount intake.","A label's serving size does not change the actual amount consumed."]],
  ["lactase-mechanism","carbohydrate",3,"After milk, a lactase-deficient patient develops gas and osmotic diarrhea. Which unabsorbed substrate explains both effects?","Lactose retained in the intestinal lumen",["Fructose released from lactose","Intact glycogen from milk","Bile salts made by lactase"],"Unhydrolyzed lactose retains water and is fermented by colonic microbes. Lactase normally cleaves it into glucose and galactose.",["Lactose contains glucose and galactose, not fructose.","Milk carbohydrate is chiefly lactose rather than glycogen.","Lactase is a brush-border enzyme and does not produce bile salts."]],
  ["fiber-cholesterol","carbohydrate",4,"Two diets contain equal fiber grams, but one contains more viscous soluble fiber. Why might its LDL-lowering effect differ?","Viscosity can alter absorption and increase fecal bile-acid loss",["All fiber molecules act identically per gram","Fiber is absorbed intact and dissolves circulating LDL","Viscous fiber converts LDL directly into HDL"],"Fiber effects depend on physicochemical properties, including viscosity and fermentability, not simply the gram total. Increased bile-acid loss can promote hepatic cholesterol use.",["The fiber category includes chemically and physiologically different materials.","Dietary fiber is not absorbed intact into blood to dissolve LDL.","The mechanism is not direct conversion of one lipoprotein into another."]],
  ["fiber-lignin","carbohydrate",3,"A list classifies every dietary fiber as a polysaccharide. Which item exposes the error?","Lignin",["Cellulose","Pectin","Glycogen"],"Lignin contributes to dietary fiber but is not a carbohydrate polysaccharide; fiber is a functional category.",["Cellulose is a polysaccharide, although human enzymes do not digest its beta linkages.","Pectin is a carbohydrate polymer.","Glycogen is a storage polysaccharide, not the noncarbohydrate-fiber exception."]],
  ["glycemic-load","carbohydrate",4,"A food has a glycemic index of 60 and a portion supplies 20 g of available carbohydrate. What is the approximate glycemic load?","12",["3","60","1200"],"Glycemic load = GI × available carbohydrate grams / 100 = 60 × 20 / 100 = 12. The portion matters, unlike GI alone.",["Dividing GI by carbohydrate grams is not the glycemic-load formula.","60 is the GI, not the portion-adjusted glycemic load.","The GI scale requires division by 100."]],
  ["essential-fat-pair","lipids",3,"Which pair supplies the two conventionally essential parent fatty acids?","Linoleic acid and alpha-linolenic acid",["Oleic acid and stearic acid","Cholesterol and phosphatidylcholine","EPA and palmitic acid"],"Linoleic acid is the omega-6 parent and alpha-linolenic acid the omega-3 parent. Humans cannot introduce the required double bonds de novo.",["Oleic and stearic acids can ordinarily be synthesized by the body.","These are lipid classes/molecules, not the two essential parent fatty acids.","Palmitic acid is not an essential fatty acid; EPA is a longer-chain omega-3."]],
  ["trans-chemistry","lipids",3,"A fatty acid has one trans carbon–carbon double bond. Which description is chemically correct?","It is unsaturated despite relatively straight packing",["It is saturated because it is straight","It has no double bonds","It must be an essential omega-3 fatty acid"],"Unsaturation refers to double bonds; cis/trans describes their geometry. A trans bond does not become a saturated bond.",["Molecular shape does not remove the double bond.","The stem explicitly contains a double bond.","Trans geometry does not establish omega position or essentiality."]],
  ["phospholipid-amphipathic","lipids",3,"Why can phosphatidylcholine help organize a membrane interface?","It combines a polar head with nonpolar fatty-acid tails",["It consists only of free cholesterol","Its entire molecule is equally water soluble","It is a single unesterified fatty acid"],"Phospholipids are amphipathic. Polar heads interact with aqueous surroundings while hydrophobic tails associate away from water.",["Phosphatidylcholine is not cholesterol.","The hydrophobic tails are not freely water soluble.","A complete phospholipid is more than one free fatty acid."]],
  ["omega-oil-cultivar","lipids",4,"A past-paper rule says sunflower oil is predominantly linoleic acid. A product is labeled high-oleic sunflower oil. How should it be classified?","Use the cultivar-specific composition; the old generalization does not apply",["Call oleic acid an omega-3","Ignore the label because all sunflower oils are identical","Assume all of its fatty acids are saturated"],"Traditional high-linoleic and high-oleic cultivars differ substantially. Oleic acid is monounsaturated omega-9, so food-name rules need qualification.",["Oleic acid is omega-9, not omega-3.","Cultivar selection changes fatty-acid composition.","High-oleic oil is rich in a monounsaturated fatty acid."]],
  ["protein-rda-calculation","protein",3,"Using the conventional healthy-adult RDA, how much protein should a 70-kg adult plan for daily?","56 g",["8.75 g","70 mg","87.5 g"],"0.8 g/kg/day × 70 kg = 56 g/day. This planning reference is not a universal prescription for pregnancy, dialysis or severe illness.",["This divides or misapplies the coefficient instead of multiplying 0.8 by 70.","The requirement is measured in grams, not this tiny milligram amount.","This uses 70/0.8 rather than 70 × 0.8."]],
  ["pku-conditional","protein",4,"In classic phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency, why can tyrosine become conditionally essential?","Its usual synthesis from phenylalanine is impaired",["Tyrosine is converted into phenylalanine too rapidly","All essential amino acids must be eliminated","Tyrosine contains no nitrogen"],"The blocked phenylalanine-to-tyrosine pathway increases reliance on dietary tyrosine. Phenylalanine must be controlled, not universally eliminated, because it remains essential.",["The blocked forward synthesis, not accelerated reverse conversion, is central.","Growth requires essential amino acids, including a controlled phenylalanine supply.","Tyrosine is a nitrogen-containing amino acid."]],
  ["nitrogen-balance","protein",4,"An adult takes in 12 g nitrogen daily and loses an estimated 16 g through measured and other routes. Which interpretation fits?","Negative nitrogen balance consistent with net protein loss",["Positive balance of 4 g nitrogen","Equilibrium because both values are positive","A dedicated inert protein store is increasing"],"Balance = intake − losses = −4 g nitrogen/day. Catabolic illness or inadequate intake can produce negative balance; assess the cause.",["The sign is reversed: losses exceed intake.","Equilibrium requires equal intake and loss, not merely positive numbers.","There is no inert storage depot analogous to adipose triglyceride; body proteins have functions."]],
  ["complementary-protein","protein",3,"Why can legumes and grains complement one another in a varied diet?","Their limiting indispensable amino-acid patterns differ",["Plant foods contain no protein until mixed","Mixing them creates vitamin B12","Every complementary pair must be eaten in the same mouthful"],"Different plant proteins can improve the overall indispensable-amino-acid pattern across the diet. Adequate total intake and variety matter.",["Both food groups already contain protein.","Complementation does not create a reliable B12 source.","A varied dietary pattern need not place the foods in the same mouthful."]],
  ["bile-malabsorption","digestion",4,"A patient develops prolonged cholestasis, steatorrhea and easy bruising. Which deficiency mechanism is most coherent?","Impaired micellar fat absorption with reduced vitamin K availability",["Bile normally hydrolyzes all peptide bonds","Vitamin K is normally absorbed only in the stomach","Steatorrhea proves isolated vitamin B1 deficiency"],"Reduced bile delivery impairs fat and fat-soluble vitamin absorption. Vitamin K deficiency can compromise coagulation; other causes of bleeding still require assessment.",["Bile salts facilitate emulsification/micelles; proteases hydrolyze peptide bonds.","Vitamin K absorption is intestinal and related to fat handling.","Steatorrhea points to fat maldigestion/malabsorption, not isolated thiamin deficiency."]],
  ["absorption-route","digestion",3,"Which absorbed nutrient is normally packaged into chylomicrons before entering lymph?","Long-chain dietary lipid",["Glucose","Most amino acids","Galactose"],"Enterocytes re-esterify long-chain lipid and package it into chylomicrons entering lacteals. Most sugars and amino acids enter portal blood.",["Glucose normally enters portal blood rather than chylomicrons.","Most amino acids enter the portal circulation.","Galactose is a monosaccharide entering portal blood."]],
  ["ileal-b12","digestion",4,"Despite adequate B12 intake, a patient with substantial terminal-ileal disease develops deficiency. Which step is compromised?","Uptake of the intrinsic-factor–B12 complex",["Synthesis of B12 by pancreatic lipase","Conversion of folate into B12","Gastric absorption of all dietary B12"],"The intrinsic-factor–B12 complex is taken up in the terminal ileum. Adequate intake does not ensure absorption.",["Pancreatic lipase digests fat and does not synthesize B12.","Folate is not converted into B12.","The main receptor-mediated absorption site is terminal ileum, not stomach."]],
  ["pancreatic-fat","digestion",4,"Which finding best explains poor fat absorption in severe exocrine pancreatic insufficiency despite normal bile delivery?","Insufficient lipase-mediated hydrolysis of triglyceride",["Loss of the liver's ability to make all glucose","Failure of lactose to form micelles","Failure of the stomach to synthesize bile"],"Bile-assisted micelles cannot fully compensate for lack of pancreatic digestion. Fat maldigestion can reduce energy and fat-soluble vitamin availability.",["This is not the digestive defect specified.","Lactose uses disaccharidase digestion, not lipid micelles.","Bile is produced by the liver, not the stomach."]],
  ["meal-energy","energy",3,"A meal provides 50 g carbohydrate, 25 g protein and 20 g fat, with no alcohol. What is its calculated energy?","480 kcal",["380 kcal","855 kcal","95 kcal"],"50 × 4 + 25 × 4 + 20 × 9 = 200 + 100 + 180 = 480 kcal.",["380 kcal misses part of the protein/fat contribution.","855 kcal incorrectly applies 9 kcal/g to all 95 g.","95 g is nutrient mass, not energy in kcal."]],
  ["fat-energy-percent","energy",3,"A 2,400-kcal diet contains 80 g fat. What percentage of energy comes from fat?","30%",["3.3%","13.3%","80%"],"80 × 9 = 720 kcal from fat; 720/2400 × 100 = 30%.",["This divides grams by kcal without converting fat mass to energy.","This applies 4 rather than 9 kcal/g to fat.","Fat grams are not automatically a percentage."]],
  ["basal-measurement","energy",4,"Which measurement condition best approximates basal metabolic rate rather than activity expenditure or the thermic effect of a meal?","Awake, resting, postabsorptive and thermoneutral",["Immediately after a large meal","During a graded treadmill test","Shivering in a cold room after exercise"],"Basal conditions minimize recent food, voluntary activity and thermal stress while the person is awake and at rest.",["A recent meal adds thermic expenditure.","Exercise adds voluntary activity expenditure.","Shivering and recent exercise elevate expenditure above basal conditions."]],
  ["calorimetry-distinction","energy",4,"An instrument estimates resting expenditure using oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production. What is being used?","Indirect calorimetry",["Direct heat calorimetry","A food-frequency questionnaire","A body-weight-only prediction equation"],"Respiratory gas exchange is the basis of indirect calorimetry. Direct calorimetry measures heat; equations predict rather than directly measure the individual's gas exchange.",["Direct calorimetry measures heat transfer, not respiratory gas exchange.","A questionnaire describes intake patterns, not measured metabolic expenditure.","A prediction equation does not collect oxygen and carbon dioxide measurements."]],
  ["bmi-units","assessment",3,"An adult weighs 81 kg and is 180 cm tall. What is the BMI?","25.0 kg/m²",["0.0025 kg/m²","45.0 kg/m²","20.0 kg/m²"],"Convert 180 cm to 1.80 m, then calculate 81/(1.8²) = 25.0 kg/m².",["This uses centimeters directly in a formula requiring meters.","This divides by height rather than height squared.","20 does not follow from the given mass and squared height."]],
  ["albumin-inflammation","assessment",4,"A patient with acute inflammation has low albumin. Why is it unsafe to infer the exact daily protein intake from that result?","Inflammation, distribution and illness affect albumin independently of intake",["Albumin is always unchanged by illness","Albumin measures only yesterday's protein grams","Low albumin proves dietary deficiency even with major fluid shifts"],"Albumin and prealbumin are strongly affected by the acute-phase response and other illness factors. Nutritional assessment combines intake, weight history, examination and function.",["Inflammation and illness can substantially change albumin.","There is no direct conversion from albumin concentration to yesterday's protein grams.","Fluid balance and disease prevent that isolated inference."]],
  ["waist-bmi","assessment",3,"Two adults have the same BMI but markedly different waist circumferences. What does BMI fail to capture?","Regional adiposity and body composition",["Their measured heights","Their measured weights","The arithmetic definition of mass divided by height squared"],"BMI does not distinguish central fat, peripheral fat or muscle. Waist and other assessments add information; BMI alone is not a full risk assessment.",["Height is already part of BMI.","Weight is already part of BMI.","The formula is known; its limitation is what tissues/distribution it does not describe."]],
  ["diet-recall-bias","assessment",3,"A patient records one unusually healthy day and asks whether it proves their usual intake is adequate. What is the best response?","Atypical-day and reporting bias require assessment of the longer-term pattern",["One day exactly measures usual intake","A food record measures intestinal absorption directly","Recording food eliminates portion-size errors"],"Records and recalls provide dietary evidence but can be affected by day-to-day variation, omissions and portion error. They do not directly measure absorption.",["Usual intake cannot generally be inferred from one atypical day.","Food records measure reported consumption, not nutrient uptake.","Portion estimates can remain inaccurate in a written record."]],
  ["edema-masks-wasting","malnutrition",4,"A severely undernourished child gains scale weight while bilateral pitting edema worsens. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","Retained fluid may conceal continued tissue loss",["The weight gain proves restoration of muscle","Edema excludes malnutrition","BMI alone now demonstrates normal nutritional status"],"Fluid retention can increase measured mass despite loss of fat and muscle. Nutritional edema requires careful assessment and supervised care.",["Scale weight cannot separate added fluid from new tissue.","Edema is a recognized severe-malnutrition presentation.","BMI is distorted by fluid retention and is not a stand-alone assessment."]],
  ["refeeding-phosphate","malnutrition",4,"After prolonged starvation, carbohydrate refeeding is followed by a fall in circulating phosphate, potassium and magnesium. What explains this pattern?","Insulin-driven intracellular shifts with increased anabolic demand",["Carbohydrate has no hormonal effect","All three electrolytes become unnecessary during feeding","Vitamin A is converted into phosphate"],"Refeeding raises insulin and metabolic demands, shifting electrolytes into cells and increasing thiamin requirements. High-risk refeeding needs clinical monitoring.",["Carbohydrate stimulates insulin and changes substrate handling.","Refeeding increases cellular needs rather than making electrolytes unnecessary.","Vitamin A cannot supply elemental phosphate in this way."]],
  ["wasting-stunting","malnutrition",3,"A child's height for age is low, but weight for height is not low. Which descriptive term fits the linear-growth deficit?","Stunting",["Wasting","Adult central obesity","Positive nitrogen balance"],"Stunting describes low height for age. Wasting describes low weight for height; the patterns can coexist but are not interchangeable.",["Weight for height is not low in the stem.","An adult fat-distribution label does not describe childhood linear growth.","Nitrogen balance cannot be calculated from these anthropometric findings."]],
  ["kwashiorkor-complexity","malnutrition",4,"Which statement best updates the simplistic claim that kwashiorkor is caused only by isolated protein deprivation?","Nutritional edema involves interacting metabolic, inflammatory and other factors",["Total energy and infections never matter","All edema proves kwashiorkor","Protein status is irrelevant to malnutrition"],"Kwashiorkor/nutritional edema is not fully explained by one nutrient alone. Diet, infection, oxidative and metabolic changes can interact, and other causes of edema must be considered.",["This excludes important interacting determinants.","Cardiac, renal, hepatic and other disorders can also cause edema.","Rejecting an isolated-cause model does not make protein unimportant."]],
  ["retinal-retinoic","vitamin-a",3,"A question specifically asks for the vitamin-A derivative participating directly in the light-sensitive visual pigment. Which form fits?","11-cis retinal",["Retinoic acid","Retinol-binding protein","A retinyl ester stored in liver"],"11-cis retinal is the visual chromophore that isomerizes after light absorption. Retinoic acid instead mediates gene-regulatory effects.",["Retinoic acid is not the visual-pigment chromophore.","This is a carrier protein, not the vitamin derivative used in the pigment.","Storage esters are not the direct light-sensitive form."]],
  ["retinol-pregnancy","vitamin-a",4,"A pregnant patient thinks megadoses of preformed vitamin A must improve fetal development because normal amounts are essential. What is the key correction?","Excess preformed vitamin A can be toxic and teratogenic",["Essential nutrients are harmless at every dose","All green vegetables should be avoided","Vitamin A only affects the mother's eyes"],"Essentiality does not remove dose-related toxicity. Excess preformed retinoids differ from normal dietary intake and from ordinary consumption of carotenoid-rich vegetables.",["Deficiency and excess can both harm.","The concern is not a blanket ban on nutritious vegetables.","Vitamin A participates in differentiation and development beyond vision."]],
  ["raevsunits","vitamin-a",4,"Why should equal microgram masses of retinol and beta-carotene not automatically be treated as equal vitamin-A activity?","Conversion and bioavailability differ; use retinol activity equivalents",["Retinol has no vitamin activity","Every carotenoid is a preformed retinoid","Micrograms and milligrams are the same unit"],"RAE accounts for differences in activity from preformed vitamin A versus provitamin carotenoids. Chemical mass alone does not establish equal activity.",["Retinol is an active vitamin-A form.","Carotenoids are not all preformed retinoids or even all provitamin A.","A milligram is 1,000 micrograms."]],
  ["vitamin-a-deficiency-pattern","vitamin-a",3,"Impaired dark adaptation accompanies conjunctival dryness in a person with prolonged poor intake. Which nutrient's deficiency best links the findings?","Vitamin A",["Vitamin K","Sodium","Niacin"],"Vitamin A supports the visual cycle and epithelial integrity. The combination supports assessment for deficiency, although clinical diagnosis requires context.",["Vitamin K is chiefly associated with coagulation.","Sodium disorders do not explain this classic visual/epithelial pattern.","Niacin deficiency classically produces pellagra rather than xerophthalmia."]],
  ["vitamin-d-activation","vitamin-d",4,"Which conversion is the principal renal ACTIVATING step in vitamin D metabolism?","25-hydroxyvitamin D to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D by 1-alpha-hydroxylation",["Vitamin D to 25-hydroxyvitamin D as the principal hepatic step","Formation of 24-hydroxylated catabolic metabolites as the activating step","Retinol to retinal"],"Renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase acts on an already 25-hydroxylated substrate. Specifying activation avoids the ambiguity of asking merely which hydroxylation can occur in kidneys.",["This describes the principal liver step, not renal activation.","24-hydroxylation chiefly supports catabolic regulation rather than the activating step asked here.","Retinol/retinal belongs to vitamin A metabolism."]],
  ["vitamin-d-status","vitamin-d",4,"Why is 25-hydroxyvitamin D usually a better status marker than calcitriol?","It better reflects the supply/stores and has a longer circulating half-life",["Calcitriol can never be measured","Calcitriol always falls before stores fall","25-hydroxyvitamin D is unrelated to intake or synthesis"],"Calcitriol is tightly regulated and may remain maintained despite low stores. 25-hydroxyvitamin D is generally used to assess status; assay and threshold interpretation still matter.",["Measurement is possible, but it answers a different question.","Regulation can maintain calcitriol even when stores are low.","25-hydroxyvitamin D reflects dietary and synthesized vitamin D supply."]],
  ["osteomalacia-osteoporosis","vitamin-d",4,"Which distinction separates osteomalacia from uncomplicated osteoporosis?","Defective osteoid mineralization versus reduced normally mineralized bone mass",["Both mean identical defects in collagen synthesis","Osteoporosis occurs only in growing children","Osteomalacia is simply another name for low BMI"],"Osteomalacia involves impaired mineralization of adult osteoid; osteoporosis chiefly involves reduced bone mass and structural deterioration with generally normal mineralization of the remaining matrix.",["The two conditions are not identical collagen defects.","Osteoporosis is not confined to childhood.","BMI does not define mineralization."]],
  ["d-iu-conversion","vitamin-d",3,"A vitamin D label provides 20 micrograms daily. Using 1 microgram = 40 IU, what amount is this?","800 IU",["500 IU","20 IU","0.5 IU"],"20 × 40 = 800 IU, the DRI reference amount for adults over age 70. A study reference is not an individualized prescription.",["500 does not follow from the stated conversion.","Micrograms and IU are not numerically identical.","Dividing by 40 reverses the stated conversion."]],
  ["e-se-antioxidants","vitamin-ek",4,"Which pairing correctly distinguishes complementary antioxidant roles?","Vitamin E interrupts membrane lipid chains; selenium-dependent enzymes remove peroxides",["Vitamin E creates thrombin; selenium binds retinal","Both act only by increasing calcium absorption","Selenium is itself a fat-soluble vitamin"],"Vitamin E provides lipid-phase chain-breaking antioxidant activity; selenium is part of selenoproteins such as glutathione peroxidases. Adequacy is not an argument for megadoses.",["These are not their principal antioxidant mechanisms.","Calcium absorption is not the shared mechanism described.","Selenium is an element, not a vitamin."]],
  ["vitamin-k-carboxylation","vitamin-ek",4,"Fat malabsorption reduces vitamin K availability. Which biochemical process is directly threatened?","Gamma-carboxylation of selected coagulation proteins",["Hydroxylation of collagen proline by vitamin C","Conversion of tryptophan to niacin","Synthesis of the retinal chromophore"],"Vitamin K is required for gamma-carboxylation of specific glutamate residues in proteins including several coagulation factors, permitting normal calcium-dependent function.",["Collagen hydroxylation is a vitamin-C-related process.","This is not the vitamin-K-dependent reaction.","The visual chromophore is vitamin-A-related."]],
  ["e-deficiency-malabsorption","vitamin-ek",3,"Severe chronic fat malabsorption is followed by hemolytic and neurological abnormalities. Which antioxidant vitamin warrants particular consideration?","Vitamin E",["Vitamin B1 only","Vitamin C only","Folate only"],"Severe vitamin E deficiency can compromise membrane protection and neurological function. Fat malabsorption also warrants assessment of other fat-soluble vitamins.",["Thiamin has neurological roles but does not provide the lipid-phase protection specified.","Vitamin C is water soluble and not the best fit for the fat-malabsorption/hemolytic clue.","Folate supports nucleotide synthesis, not this antioxidant pattern."]],
  ["k-anticoagulant-consistency","vitamin-ek",4,"A person taking a vitamin-K antagonist plans to remove every green vegetable permanently. Which general principle better fits nutrition counseling?","Maintain a consistent vitamin-K intake and coordinate changes with the clinician",["Abruptly alternate no vegetables with large amounts","Ignore medication because foods never interact","Take very high-dose vitamin K without informing the prescriber"],"Vitamin-K-antagonist control is affected by changes in vitamin K intake. A consistent nutritious pattern and clinical coordination are preferable to unsupervised extremes.",["Large fluctuations can destabilize the medication effect.","Food and supplement intake can affect medication response.","Unsupervised high doses can oppose the intended anticoagulant effect."]],
  ["thiamin-carb-load","b-vitamins",4,"Why is thiamin especially relevant when a severely undernourished patient begins receiving substantial carbohydrate?","Carbohydrate oxidation increases demand for thiamin-dependent reactions",["Thiamin supplies 9 kcal/g","Thiamin is stored in a large inert depot for decades","Carbohydrate prevents all thiamin-deficiency complications"],"Thiamin-dependent enzymes support oxidative carbohydrate metabolism. Refeeding can expose limited reserves, so at-risk patients require supervised management.",["Vitamins do not supply dietary energy in this way.","Thiamin reserves are limited, not a decades-long inert store.","Carbohydrate loading can worsen the consequences of inadequate thiamin."]],
  ["niacin-pellagra","b-vitamins",3,"A nutritionally vulnerable patient has photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea and cognitive changes. Which vitamin pathway is most directly suggested?","Niacin / NAD and NADP",["Riboflavin / visual retinal","Vitamin K / collagen hydroxylation","Vitamin D / hemoglobin iron transport"],"The classic pellagra pattern is associated with niacin deficiency. Niacin contributes to NAD/NADP, and tryptophan can contribute to niacin equivalents.",["Riboflavin forms FMN/FAD, not retinal.","Vitamin K does not mediate collagen hydroxylation.","Vitamin D is not the transport molecule for hemoglobin iron."]],
  ["riboflavin-coenzymes","b-vitamins",3,"A question asks which vitamin forms FMN and FAD rather than NAD and NADP. Which is correct?","Riboflavin",["Niacin","Folate","Vitamin A"],"Riboflavin supplies the flavin component of FMN and FAD. Niacin instead contributes to NAD/NADP.",["Niacin is the NAD/NADP answer, not the flavin answer.","Folate carries one-carbon units rather than forming flavins.","Vitamin A produces retinoid forms rather than flavin coenzymes."]],
  ["b6-transamination","b-vitamins",4,"Which coenzyme-vitamin pairing best explains a role in amino-acid transamination?","Pyridoxal phosphate — vitamin B6",["Retinal — vitamin A","Calcitriol — vitamin D","Phylloquinone — vitamin K"],"Pyridoxal phosphate supports many amino-acid reactions, including transamination. Vitamin B6 also participates in transsulfuration.",["Retinal is central to the visual cycle.","Calcitriol is a calcium/phosphate-regulating hormone.","Vitamin K supports protein gamma-carboxylation."]],
  ["b12-mma","folate-b12",4,"Two patients have megaloblastic anemia and elevated homocysteine. With renal function accounted for, which additional elevation favors B12 deficiency over isolated folate deficiency?","Methylmalonic acid",["Serum sodium","Serum triglyceride as a specific marker","Retinol-binding protein as a specific marker"],"B12 is required by methylmalonyl-CoA mutase as well as methionine synthase. Methylmalonic acid can rise in B12 deficiency; renal impairment can complicate interpretation.",["Sodium is not a specific discriminator for these deficiencies.","Triglycerides are not a specific B12-versus-folate test.","Retinol-binding protein does not specifically distinguish these B-vitamin deficiencies."]],
  ["folate-masks-b12","folate-b12",4,"Why should neurological symptoms accompanying megaloblastic anemia prompt B12 assessment before relying on folate alone?","Folate may improve anemia while untreated B12-related neurological injury continues",["Folate directly replaces B12 in every enzyme","Neurological injury is impossible in B12 deficiency","Folate blocks all red-cell production"],"Correction of the hematological picture does not guarantee correction of B12-dependent neurological problems. Investigate the cause rather than treating a blood count in isolation.",["The two vitamins are not interchangeable at all B12-dependent enzymes.","Neurological disease is an important manifestation of B12 deficiency.","Folate supports nucleotide synthesis and can improve deficient erythropoiesis."]],
  ["homocysteine-exits","folate-b12",4,"Which map correctly pairs the two major homocysteine pathways with their vitamin requirements?","Remethylation: folate/B12; transsulfuration: B6",["Remethylation: vitamin A; transsulfuration: vitamin D","Both depend exclusively on vitamin C","B12 converts cysteine directly into folate"],"Homocysteine can be remethylated to methionine with folate/B12 support or enter the B6-dependent pathway toward cysteine. Elevation therefore has multiple potential causes.",["Retinoids and vitamin D do not define these pathways.","Vitamin C is not the exclusive cofactor for these routes.","This is not the biochemical relationship among these molecules."]],
  ["b12-unit-error","folate-b12",3,"A healthy-adult dietary reference table is being checked. Which B12 value has the correct magnitude and units?","2.4 micrograms/day",["2.4 milligrams/day","240 milligrams/day","2.4 grams/day"],"The healthy-adult B12 RDA is 2.4 micrograms/day. Supplement/treatment doses may differ and must not be confused with dietary requirements.",["2.4 mg is 1,000 times 2.4 micrograms.","240 mg is far beyond the dietary-reference magnitude.","Grams are the wrong magnitude for the B12 RDA."]],
  ["c-collagen","vitamin-c",3,"Poor wound healing, perifollicular hemorrhages and gingival changes follow a highly restricted diet. Which mechanism links the findings?","Impaired vitamin-C-dependent collagen hydroxylation",["Failure to form retinal from vitamin K","Loss of selenium from hemoglobin","Excess essential fatty-acid synthesis"],"Vitamin C supports collagen hydroxylation and connective-tissue integrity. Deficiency can produce scurvy; bleeding still requires appropriate assessment.",["Retinal belongs to vitamin A metabolism, not vitamin K.","Hemoglobin contains iron; this is not a selenium-loss mechanism.","Humans cannot synthesize the essential parent fatty acids de novo, and this is not the connective-tissue mechanism."]],
  ["c-nonheme","vitamin-c",3,"Which meal change most directly promotes absorption of nonheme iron from legumes?","Add a vitamin-C-rich food at the same meal",["Drink strong tea with the meal","Remove all protein-containing foods","Replace the meal with water"],"Vitamin C can enhance nonheme iron availability and absorption within the meal. It does not remove the need to investigate substantial iron deficiency.",["Tea polyphenols can inhibit nonheme iron absorption.","Removing all protein is not the mechanism and can worsen dietary adequacy.","Water provides no replacement iron or nutrients."]],
  ["c-cooking","vitamin-c",3,"Why can prolonged storage and boiling reduce the vitamin C contribution of vegetables?","Vitamin C is vulnerable to degradation and loss into cooking water",["Vitamin C becomes a heat-stable mineral","Boiling always doubles vitamin C concentration","Vitamin C is an insoluble structural fat"],"Vitamin C is water soluble and vulnerable to oxygen/heat-related losses. Preparation and use of cooking liquid affect retained intake.",["A vitamin cannot be transformed into a mineral by cooking.","Concentration may change with water loss, but boiling does not create vitamin C.","Vitamin C is not a lipid."]],
  ["c-carnitine-link","vitamin-c",4,"Which vitamin links connective-tissue hydroxylation with hydroxylation steps in carnitine synthesis?","Vitamin C",["Vitamin B12","Vitamin K","Vitamin A"],"Ascorbate supports several hydroxylation reactions, including collagen and carnitine-related pathways; its functions extend beyond generic antioxidant labeling.",["B12 supports methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, not this hydroxylation pairing.","Vitamin K supports gamma-carboxylation rather than this pairing.","Vitamin A acts in vision and transcriptional regulation."]],
  ["iron-ferritin-inflammation","iron",4,"Serum iron is low in chronic inflammation, but ferritin is normal/high. Which interpretation is most defensible?","Inflammation can sequester iron and raise ferritin; concurrent absolute deficiency remains possible",["High ferritin always excludes iron deficiency","Ferritin is the main iron-export channel","Iron is unnecessary during inflammation"],"Inflammation can increase hepcidin and ferritin while reducing available iron. Interpret ferritin alongside clinical context and other indices.",["Ferritin is an acute-phase reactant and can obscure depleted stores.","Ferroportin is the export protein; ferritin stores iron.","Iron remains necessary for erythropoiesis and other functions."]],
  ["iron-transport-storage","iron",3,"Which pairing correctly distinguishes the principal plasma transport protein from a storage protein for iron?","Transferrin — transport; ferritin — storage",["Ferritin — transport; transferrin — storage","Albumin — intracellular iron export; hepcidin — oxygen transport","Ceruloplasmin — hemoglobin; calcium — ferritin"],"Transferrin carries iron in plasma; ferritin stores it. Hepcidin regulates availability partly through ferroportin.",["This reverses the major roles.","These are not the functions of albumin and hepcidin.","Ceruloplasmin and calcium do not substitute for the listed iron proteins."]],
  ["microcytosis-differential","iron",4,"A microcytic blood count is found in a person with a family history of thalassemia. Why not immediately attribute it to poor iron intake?","Inherited globin disorders also cause microcytosis; iron status must be assessed",["All microcytosis proves dietary iron deficiency","Thalassemia is caused by insufficient dietary iron","An iron-rich meal rules out all inherited disease"],"Microcytosis is a pattern, not a single diagnosis. Iron deficiency and thalassemia can occur separately or together; evidence should guide supplementation.",["Several disorders can produce microcytosis.","Thalassemia is inherited impairment of globin synthesis.","A meal cannot exclude a genetic disorder."]],
  ["iron-blood-loss","iron",4,"An adult develops depleted iron stores despite reporting an adequate diet. Which mechanism must not be dismissed?","Chronic occult gastrointestinal blood loss",["Food intake guarantees zero iron loss","All iron is permanently recycled without losses","Only vitamin A deficiency can lower iron stores"],"Absorbed intake must balance losses. Occult bleeding, malabsorption and other causes may produce deficiency despite apparently adequate food intake.",["Adequate reported intake does not prevent pathological blood loss.","Iron is recycled extensively but can leave the body through blood and other losses.","There are multiple mechanisms of iron depletion beyond vitamin A status."]],
  ["zinc-copper-excess","minerals",4,"Prolonged high-dose zinc supplementation is followed by a second trace-element deficiency. Which interaction should be considered?","Impaired copper availability",["Zinc is converted directly to copper","Zinc guarantees unlimited iron absorption","All trace elements share one harmless excess threshold"],"Excess zinc can interfere with copper absorption/availability. Dose matters even when a nutrient is essential.",["Biological metabolism does not convert one element into another.","Zinc excess does not guarantee improved iron absorption.","Trace elements have different kinetics and toxicities."]],
  ["iodine-thyroid","minerals",3,"Which deficiency directly limits synthesis of thyroid hormones and can threaten fetal neurodevelopment?","Iodine",["Fluoride","Chromium","Chloride as the unique thyroid precursor"],"Iodine is incorporated into thyroid hormones. Both inadequate and excessive iodine exposure can disturb thyroid physiology.",["Fluoride is chiefly associated with dental mineralization, not thyroid-hormone synthesis.","Chromium is not the element incorporated into thyroid hormones.","Chloride does not replace iodine in thyroid hormone."]],
  ["ceruloplasmin-storage","minerals",4,"A handout says copper is 'transported and stored by ceruloplasmin.' What needs correction?","Ceruloplasmin is a major circulating copper-containing protein, not the principal storage depot",["Ceruloplasmin transports only sodium","Copper is stored exclusively in red-cell hemoglobin","Copper has no role in iron handling"],"The handout combines transport-related and storage concepts. Copper homeostasis involves hepatic and cellular handling; ceruloplasmin also supports iron metabolism.",["Ceruloplasmin is a copper-containing protein, not a sodium-only transporter.","Hemoglobin contains iron, not an exclusive copper-storage depot.","Copper-dependent enzymes participate in iron handling."]],
  ["water-electrolyte-context","minerals",4,"A student advises every patient with kidney disease to eat unlimited potassium-rich foods and drink extra water. What is the main problem?","Fluid and potassium advice depends on renal function, treatment and measured status",["Potassium has no physiological function","All fruit is universally prohibited","Extra water always restores glomerular function"],"Potassium and fluid recommendations must account for residual renal function, medications, dialysis and laboratory/clinical findings. Neither unrestricted intake nor blanket prohibition is sound.",["Potassium is a major intracellular ion essential for excitability.","The appropriate food pattern is individualized rather than a universal fruit ban.","Additional water does not universally restore filtration and can worsen overload."]],
  ["pregnancy-timing-folate","pregnancy",4,"Why does folic-acid prevention begin before conception rather than waiting for a late-pregnancy appointment?","Neural-tube development occurs very early, often before pregnancy is recognized",["Folate has no function after conception","The neural tube first forms in the third trimester","A large late dose reverses every established defect"],"Periconceptional folate adequacy targets early development. It reduces risk but cannot guarantee prevention or reverse every established malformation.",["Folate remains important for maternal and fetal cell division.","Neural-tube development is an early embryonic event.","Prevention is not equivalent to late reversal of established anatomy."]],
  ["pregnancy-energy-increment","pregnancy",3,"Under the book's general convention, a person with a 2,000-kcal baseline enters the third trimester. Which approximate total includes the usual 450-kcal increment?","2,450 kcal/day",["450 kcal/day","4,000 kcal/day","2,000 + 450 grams of food/day"],"The increment is added to baseline: 2,000 + 450 = 2,450 kcal/day. Actual needs depend on the individual and pregnancy course.",["450 kcal is the increment, not the entire daily requirement.","'Eat for two' does not imply doubling total energy.","Energy increments use kcal, not unspecified food grams."]],
  ["pregnancy-calcium-age","pregnancy",3,"Which distinction is correct for calcium reference intakes in pregnancy?","Adult pregnancy commonly uses 1,000 mg/day; adolescent pregnancy uses 1,300 mg/day",["All pregnant people require exactly twice their prepregnancy calcium RDA","Age never affects the reference","Calcium can be replaced gram-for-gram by vitamin D"],"The calcium reference follows age: adolescence has a higher requirement. Pregnancy does not automatically double every nutrient RDA.",["Requirements do not follow a universal doubling rule.","Adolescent and adult reference values differ.","Vitamin D aids absorption but cannot replace elemental calcium."]],
  ["pregnancy-fish","pregnancy",4,"Which description best captures current general U.S. advice about fish during pregnancy rather than a bare number of meals?","Choose 2–3 servings weekly from lower-mercury Best Choices, considering portion size",["Avoid every fish species because allergy is inevitable","All fish species have identical mercury content","Eat any species without a portion limit"],"FDA/EPA guidance considers species, mercury category and servings; 8–12 ounces per week from lower-mercury choices is the general adult framework. Local guidance and individual context may differ.",["Blanket avoidance sacrifices potential nutritional benefits and is not the general recommendation.","Mercury content varies substantially among species.","Species and portion limits are central to the guidance."]],
  ["infant-honey","infancy",3,"Which food should not be offered before 12 months because of infant botulism risk?","Honey",["An appropriate iron-fortified infant formula","Developmentally suitable iron-rich complementary food around six months","Breast milk"],"Honey can contain Clostridium botulinum spores. Avoid it before 12 months; ordinary household preparation does not make the infant exposure appropriate.",["Suitable infant formula is a standard milk-feeding option.","Iron-rich complementary foods are important when developmentally appropriate.","Breast milk is not the honey-related exposure in this question."]],
  ["cow-milk-main-drink","infancy",3,"Why is ordinary cow's milk not recommended as the main replacement for breast milk/formula before 12 months?","Its nutrient profile is unsuitable as the main infant milk and can displace iron intake",["Every dairy exposure causes irreversible allergy","It contains no protein at all","It is nutritionally identical to infant formula"],"Main-drink recommendations differ from small amounts used in suitable foods. Ordinary cow's milk is not formulated to meet young-infant nutritional needs.",["This is an unjustified universal allergy claim.","Cow's milk contains substantial protein.","Infant formula is modified/fortified for infant needs and is not identical to ordinary cow's milk."]],
  ["infant-safe-texture","infancy",3,"A caregiver interprets an old infant-food list as permission to give whole hazelnuts. What is the key safety correction?","Whole nuts are a choking hazard; introduction must use age-appropriate preparation",["Nutrient density eliminates choking risk","Any food named in a past paper is safe in all forms","A low allergy probability guarantees swallowing safety"],"Allergy and choking are different risks. Preparation, size, texture and developmental readiness matter independently of nutritional value.",["A nutritious food can still obstruct an infant's airway.","The source list does not override preparation and developmental safety.","Allergy risk does not determine physical choking risk."]],
  ["infant-complementary-iron","infancy",4,"Why should complementary feeding around six months include suitable iron-rich foods rather than only fruit juice?","Growth-related iron needs require nutrient-dense foods; juice is not an iron-rich replacement",["Fruit juice supplies all essential nutrients","Complementary foods mean stopping all milk feeding immediately","Infants cannot develop iron deficiency"],"Around six months, developmentally suitable complementary foods add nutrients including iron while breast milk/formula remains important. Current guidance avoids juice before 12 months.",["Juice is not a nutritionally complete food.","Complementary feeding supplements rather than abruptly eliminates suitable milk feeding.","Infants can develop iron deficiency, especially when intake or stores are inadequate."]],
  ["ageing-body-composition","ageing",4,"An older adult has stable BMI but declining strength and reduced muscle area. What conclusion best fits?","Stable weight can conceal muscle loss offset by fat or fluid",["Sarcopenia is excluded by normal BMI","BMI directly measures muscle strength","Protein and micronutrient needs fall in exact proportion to weight"],"Body composition and function may worsen without a change in total mass. Assess strength, tissue reserves, diet and illness rather than relying on BMI alone.",["Sarcopenia can coexist with normal or high BMI.","BMI includes only mass and height.","Nutrient needs do not scale down uniformly with weight or energy expenditure."]],
  ["ageing-b12-acid","ageing",4,"Why can an older adult who eats meat still develop B12 deficiency?","Reduced gastric release of food-bound B12 or other malabsorption can limit availability",["Meat B12 automatically bypasses every digestive step","B12 is synthesized from dietary calcium","Age makes neurological complications impossible"],"Atrophic gastritis and other disorders can impair release or absorption of food-bound B12. Dietary intake is not equivalent to absorbed dose.",["Food-bound B12 still requires digestive processing and uptake.","Calcium is not a substrate for human B12 synthesis.","B12-related neurological problems remain relevant in older adults."]],
  ["ageing-nutrient-density","ageing",3,"With reduced activity and lean mass, an older adult needs less energy. What dietary principle follows?","Maintain nutrient density rather than proportionately reducing every nutrient",["Reduce all vitamins and protein by exactly the same percentage","Replace meals with low-nutrient sweets","Assume reduced appetite proves adequate intake"],"Energy expenditure can fall without proportional reductions in micronutrient or protein needs. Diet quality, access, chewing and swallowing require attention.",["Different nutrient requirements do not decline uniformly.","Low-nutrient calories can worsen adequacy despite meeting energy intake.","Appetite is not a reliable measurement of nutritional sufficiency."]],
  ["ageing-memory-supplements","ageing",4,"An older adult with memory concerns asks which vitamin universally prevents progression to dementia. What is the evidence-aware response?","Assess reversible causes and deficiencies; no listed vitamin is a universal prevention treatment",["Folate prevents progression in every patient","B12 prevents progression regardless of B12 status","High-dose A and D are risk-free substitutes for assessment"],"Correcting a documented deficiency is different from claiming that supplementation prevents dementia in all people. Memory complaints need clinical evaluation.",["A universal folate benefit is not established.","B12 supplementation in a replete person is not a universal dementia treatment.","High-dose fat-soluble vitamins can be harmful and do not replace assessment."]],
  ["dash-pattern","clinical-diets",3,"Which menu pattern best reflects DASH principles?","Vegetables, fruit, whole grains, legumes and suitable low-fat dairy with attention to sodium",["Unlimited processed meat if total fat is low","Only fruit juice, with no whole foods","A very high-sodium diet provided calories are restricted"],"DASH is a whole dietary pattern, not merely a low-fat or weight-loss label. Sodium and food quality matter alongside the food groups.",["Processed meat and sodium-heavy patterns do not become DASH merely by lowering total fat.","DASH includes diverse whole foods, not juice-only intake.","Calorie restriction does not cancel a high sodium intake."]],
  ["diabetes-individualize","clinical-diets",4,"A normal-weight adult with diabetes presents for dietary advice. Which first step avoids an unjustified universal prescription?","Assess the current eating pattern, activity, medications and individual goals",["Order weight loss for everyone with diabetes","Recommend unlimited nuts because they are healthy","Remove every carbohydrate-containing food"],"Diabetes nutrition is individualized. Carbohydrate quantity, quality and distribution, medication effects and overall nutritional status matter.",["Weight loss is not appropriate for every individual.","Nuts contribute energy and require portion/context considerations.","Blanket elimination is not a universal balanced plan and ignores individual treatment needs."]],
  ["dialysis-protein","clinical-diets",4,"Why may protein needs differ between nondialysis CKD and dialysis?","Dialysis-related losses and catabolism can increase replacement needs",["Dialysis converts nitrogen into essential amino acids","All renal patients need the same severe protein restriction","High creatinine alone determines a complete diet"],"Dialysis can increase amino-acid/protein losses, while nondialysis plans may moderate protein under supervision. Nutritional status, treatment and renal function must be considered.",["Dialysis removes solutes; it does not synthesize essential amino acids for the patient.","A uniform severe restriction can worsen malnutrition, especially during dialysis.","One laboratory number cannot define energy, protein, fluid and electrolyte needs."]],
  ["dialysis-fluid","clinical-diets",4,"A dialysis patient assumes fluid and minerals can be consumed without limit because the machine replaces all renal regulation. What is the best correction?","Limits depend on residual function, dialysis schedule and measured fluid/electrolyte status",["Dialysis makes every intake unrestricted","All fluid should always be eliminated","Potassium and phosphorus are never relevant to dialysis"],"Intermittent treatment and residual renal function do not duplicate continuous healthy renal regulation. An individualized plan is needed.",["Fluid and solutes can accumulate between treatments.","Total elimination of fluid is neither a universal nor practical prescription.","Potassium and phosphorus often remain important monitoring targets."]],
  ["oral-caries-erosion","oral",4,"A sugar-free acidic beverage causes enamel loss even without bacterial fermentation. Which process is most directly described?","Acid erosion rather than the defining bacterial caries mechanism",["Caries requires no acid or biofilm","Every sugar-free beverage is nonerosive","Enamel loss proves vitamin B12 deficiency"],"Direct dietary acid can erode enamel. Caries involves a biofilm-mediated acid challenge from fermentable substrate; the processes can coexist. Oral-health content is a book supplement, with course match unconfirmed.",["Biofilm and acid production are central to caries.","Absence of sugar does not remove acidity.","The stated direct acid exposure does not diagnose B12 deficiency."]],
  ["oral-frequency","oral",3,"Why can sipping a sugar-containing drink repeatedly be worse for caries risk than consuming the same amount with one meal?","Repeated exposure prolongs episodes of acid challenge",["Total sugar is the only variable that matters","Saliva has no protective role","Caries is caused solely by dietary fat"],"Exposure frequency, retention, salivary clearance and buffering influence the duration of cariogenic challenge, not just total grams.",["Frequency and oral conditions also influence risk.","Saliva helps clear, buffer and remineralize.","Fermentable carbohydrate is central to the biofilm-mediated acid mechanism."]],
  ["oral-xerostomia","oral",4,"Why does persistent xerostomia increase oral vulnerability even if sugar intake is unchanged?","Reduced saliva weakens clearance, buffering and remineralization",["Dryness increases salivary buffering automatically","Saliva only lubricates and has no mineral role","Xerostomia proves excess vitamin C"],"Saliva has multiple protective functions. Reduced flow can worsen caries risk and eating difficulties; identify the underlying cause.",["Lower flow does not automatically strengthen buffering.","Saliva also supplies buffering and mineral support.","Dry mouth has multiple causes and does not establish vitamin-C excess."]],
  ["numbers-amdr","numbers",3,"Which adult AMDR pattern keeps all three macronutrients within their conventional ranges?","Carbohydrate 50%, fat 30%, protein 20%",["Carbohydrate 80%, fat 15%, protein 5%","Carbohydrate 30%, fat 60%, protein 10%","Carbohydrate 45%, fat 5%, protein 50%"],"Conventional adult AMDRs are carbohydrate 45–65%, fat 20–35%, protein 10–35% of total energy. These are ranges, not a single obligatory distribution.",["Carbohydrate is above range, while fat and protein are below range.","Carbohydrate is below and fat above the ranges.","Fat is below and protein above the ranges."]],
  ["numbers-folate-dfe","numbers",3,"Which folate value is the total dietary reference for pregnancy, rather than the usual periconceptional folic-acid prevention amount?","600 micrograms DFE/day",["400 milligrams/day","600 grams/day","400 micrograms folic acid and 600 micrograms DFE are identical quantities in every setting"],"Pregnancy RDA is 600 micrograms dietary folate equivalents per day. DFE adjusts for bioavailability and is not interchangeable with folic-acid mass in all settings.",["Milligrams would be the wrong magnitude for this reference.","Grams are grossly inappropriate units for the folate requirement.","DFE and folic-acid mass differ because bioavailability differs."]],
  ["numbers-iron-sex","numbers",3,"In the healthy-adult DRI framework, which pair correctly distinguishes men from nonpregnant women aged 19–50?","Iron: 8 mg/day for men, 18 mg/day for women",["Iron: 18 mg/day for men, 8 mg/day for women","Iron: 27 mg/day for both","Iron: 8 micrograms/day for men, 18 micrograms/day for women"],"Usual menstrual losses contribute to the higher reference for reproductive-age women. Pregnancy and older age use different references.",["This reverses the reference values.","27 mg/day is the pregnancy RDA rather than a universal adult value.","These iron reference values are in milligrams, not micrograms."]],
  ["integrated-fat-case","integrated-recall",5,"A patient with chronic fat malabsorption develops easy bruising, bone pain and neurological symptoms. Which assessment best links the pattern without claiming a single diagnosis?","Assess multiple fat-soluble vitamins and the cause of malabsorption",["Assume every finding is caused by isolated iron deficiency","Treat only the scale weight because adequate calories exclude deficiency","Conclude that water-soluble vitamins must be the only affected class"],"Fat malabsorption can affect A, D, E and K with different clinical consequences. Symptoms remain nonspecific; assessment should link mechanisms and investigate alternatives.",["Iron deficiency does not adequately link the full malabsorption pattern.","Energy intake and micronutrient availability are not equivalent.","The direct absorption problem prominently threatens fat-soluble vitamins."]],
  ["integrated-vegan-ileum","integrated-recall",5,"A long-term vegan develops anemia and neurological symptoms, then reveals a history of ileal disease. Which reasoning is best?","Both low reliable B12 intake and impaired absorption may contribute",["Diet and malabsorption are mutually exclusive causes","Adequate folate guarantees normal B12 function","All neurological symptoms in vegans are caused by iron alone"],"A nutrient deficiency can have multiple simultaneous mechanisms. B12 requires a reliable source and an intact absorption pathway; neurological clues make isolated folate treatment inadequate.",["More than one mechanism can coexist.","Folate does not replace B12-dependent neurological/metabolic functions.","This is an unjustified single-cause assumption."]],
  ["integrated-edema-albumin","integrated-recall",5,"In an inflamed, edematous patient, BMI is normal and albumin is low. What is the soundest nutritional conclusion?","Neither BMI nor albumin alone resolves nutritional status; integrate weight history, intake, examination and function",["Normal BMI excludes all tissue depletion","Albumin exactly quantifies protein grams eaten","Edema proves the diet has supplied excess protein"],"Edema distorts weight and inflammation affects albumin. Combining complementary observations avoids two common measurement traps.",["Fluid or fat can conceal muscle loss at normal BMI.","Albumin is influenced by illness and distribution, not only dietary protein.","Edema has multiple possible mechanisms and does not establish protein excess."]],
];
